//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// A *host*, for `--host`. Not a machine: it declares the memory a program
// stages through on its way to an accelerator, and nothing else.
//
// The distinction is the whole point of the flag. `--machine` describes an
// accelerator -- what compute is placed on and what device memory it has.
// `--host` describes the machine that accelerator hangs off. Passing a machine
// file to `--host` collides on the shared vocabulary, which is the system
// telling the truth: a file declaring `HBM` and a `Topology Device` is
// describing a device.
//
//   vxc prog.vx --machine fleet/a100-80.vx --host fleet/host-x86-e5-2666v3.vx
//
// The same host under a different accelerator is the same file; the same
// accelerator on a different host likewise. That is what makes a fleet matrix a
// matrix rather than a list of pairs.
//
// See fleet/xeon-e5-2666v3.vx for the same silicon described as a *machine* --
// an x86 target that compute is placed on, rather than a host that data passes
// through. Both are true of one box, and which one a file says is decided by
// which question is being asked of it.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

// **No capacity, deliberately.**
//
// Host memory is virtual. A tensor larger than physical RAM does not fail; it
// pages, slowly. Declaring 60 GiB here and rejecting at 60 GiB would refuse
// programs that run, which is a worse answer than declining to give one -- and
// this whole directory exists to make refusals trustworthy. A device's HBM is a
// wall; a host's DRAM is a working set.
//
// So a host file states that a host *exists* and how fast its memory is. What
// the admission check gets from it is a declared endpoint for the staging seam,
// not a budget.
//
// spec: `/proc/meminfo` MemTotal = 61768396 kB (58.9 GiB) on the build box,
// recorded for reference rather than declared as a limit.
//
// UNVERIFIED bandwidth, derived rather than quoted: the E5-2600 v3 family has 4
// DDR4 channels per socket at up to 2133 MT/s, so
//   4 channels x 2133 MT/s x 8 B = 68.3 GB/s per socket, 136.5 GB/s for two.
// That is the memory controllers' peak and not an achievable copy rate; a
// memcpy moves two bytes of traffic per byte copied and cannot exceed half of
// it even in principle. Nothing rests on the figure until measured.
Memory CPU_DRAM {
  bandwidth: 136 GB/s, managed: explicit
}
